-- @description Video Cut Detection Editor extension
-- @author Saxmand
-- @version 1.6.0
-- @about
--   # Video Cut Detection Editor extension
--
--   Reads video frames and fingerprints video and audio for the "Video Cut Detection
--   Editor" script package (same repository) - install both, and restart REAPER
--   afterwards.
--
--   Named "Video Preview extension" until 1.6.0. Updating replaces the old binary; if
--   a reaper_video_preview file is still in UserPlugins it was put there by hand and
--   can be deleted.
--
--   What it adds to that script:
--
--   - Cut detection without FFmpeg. It reads a file once into a fingerprint store next
--     to the video, after which detecting again at a different sensitivity is instant
--     rather than another full pass over the file. It also distinguishes a cut from
--     fast motion, which a plain frame-difference threshold cannot.
--   - Video preview frames and cut thumbnails, decoded through AVFoundation.
--   - The Compare Videos tab, which needs this extension: it fingerprints two cuts of
--     the same film, works out which stretches moved where, and conforms a session from
--     one to the other.
--
--   The script works without it by falling back to FFmpeg for cut detection and
--   thumbnails, but the Compare Videos tab is unavailable and cut detection has to
--   re-read the whole file on every run.
--
--   macOS decodes in-process through AVFoundation, which needs nothing installed. Both
--   platforms decode on the machine's video engine rather than across every core, so
--   fingerprinting costs a fraction of the CPU a software decode does - measured at a
--   sixth of FFmpeg's on a minute of 1440x1080, for much the same wall time.
--
--   Windows decodes through Media Foundation, which is part of Windows, so nothing has
--   to be installed there either. What Media Foundation cannot read is ProRes and
--   DNxHD, which have no Windows decoder - for those the extension falls back to the
--   ffmpeg binary if one is available, and the script hands over its path.
--
--   Both platforms read ahead of the playhead rather than decoding a frame only once it
--   is asked for, which is what makes scrubbing and playback feel immediate.
--
--   The fingerprints, the cut detector and the matcher are the same code on both
--   platforms, and the frames they work from are scaled by that same code rather than
--   by each platform's decoder, so the results are identical to the bit and a store
--   built on one machine can be compared with one built on the other.
--
--   macOS: universal binary, Apple Silicon and Intel. Needs macOS 11 on Apple Silicon,
--   10.15 on Intel.
--
--   Windows: 64-bit, and Windows 10 or newer. It links the Universal CRT, which is part
--   of Windows from 10 onwards; on 7 or 8.1 that has to be installed separately through
--   Windows Update.
--
--   Source: Video/extension in this repository.
-- @provides
--   [darwin] reaper_video_cut_detection_editor.dylib
--   [win64] reaper_video_cut_detection_editor-x64.dll
-- @changelog
--   + Renamed from "Video Preview extension" to match the script it serves, since
--     nothing in the old name said where it came from. Same package, so updating
--     replaces the binary; the file in UserPlugins is now
--     reaper_video_cut_detection_editor. The ReaScript functions keep their VP_ prefix,
--     so no script that uses them needs changing.
--   + A second copy of the extension now stands down instead of registering over the
--     one already loaded. Doing so only half-took, since a function name that is taken
--     is refused, and unloading would then have stripped those names from the copy that
--     was working. Only reachable with a hand-installed leftover, but that is exactly
--     what a rename invites.
--   + Windows reads ahead now, as macOS already did, so playback and scrubbing no longer
--     decode each frame only once it is wanted. Media Foundation has no asynchronous
--     batch decode, so this is a worker thread with a reader of its own, decoding the
--     stretch the playhead is heading into: one seek and then forward reads, which is
--     what makes it worth doing, since the frames of a group of pictures come almost
--     free once its keyframe has been decoded. The window moves on at its midpoint
--     while the back half is still filling, and a seek abandons the run in flight at
--     the next frame boundary rather than mid-frame.
--   + VP_SetCacheBudget, so the cache ceiling can be set rather than fixed at 256 MB.
--     It is held to 32 MB..8 GB and returns the figure that took effect; lowering it
--     evicts down to the new figure at once.
--   + VP_CacheStats and VP_CacheClear, so the script can show what the decoded-frame
--     cache occupies and empty it on request.
--   + Fixed: thumbnails on Windows were a frame early. A frame is now chosen as the
--     nearest one to the time asked for, rather than the one whose own interval covers
--     it. A cut's time arrives rounded to the millisecond from the fingerprint store
--     while the decoder reports its own, so the two disagree slightly and in no fixed
--     direction; nearest absorbs that, where covering the time does not and lands a
--     frame early whenever the decoder's clock reads the later of the two.
--   + Fixed: cached frames from an earlier version were served instead of being decoded
--     again, so the seek fix in 1.3.1 changed nothing on screen for anyone who had
--     already viewed a file. The cache is keyed by path, time and size and nothing
--     else, so it now carries a generation that is bumped whenever a fix changes which
--     frame a time resolves to. Retired generations are deleted on startup rather than
--     left in the temporary folder.
--   + Fixed: every thumbnail and preview frame on Windows came from the wrong moment.
--     Seeking lands on the nearest keyframe at or before the time asked for and stops
--     there; the code took that frame without looking at its timestamp, on the mistaken
--     belief that Media Foundation decodes on to the requested time by itself. So each
--     picture was early by anything from nothing to a whole group of pictures, varying
--     from one to the next. Frames after the keyframe are now walked to the one
--     actually on screen at that moment, which is the frame AVFoundation returns on
--     macOS as well.
--   + Fixed: Windows thumbnails carried the padding a codec adds to reach whole
--     macroblocks, and were squashed by that much - the same cause as the fingerprint
--     fix in 1.3.0, in the separate code path that serves single frames.
--   + Fixed: a bottom-up frame had its row direction applied twice, once from the
--     buffer's own negative pitch and again from the format's flag, which read past the
--     end of the buffer.
--   + Fixed: the same file fingerprinted differently on macOS and on Windows, so cut
--     detection disagreed between them and a store built on one machine could not be
--     compared with one built on the other. Each platform had been letting its own
--     decoder shrink the frame, and VideoToolbox's scaler is not Media Foundation's:
--     measured on one file, 6.7 bits of mean Hamming difference between the two, where
--     the matcher treats 4 bits as a confident match, and only 1.7% of frames hashing
--     alike. Frames are now decoded at native size and scaled by the extension's own
--     code on every platform, which makes the fingerprints bit-identical - measured at
--     0 bits across all 1543 frames of a file decoded two independent ways - and is
--     also faster on macOS, 1.1 s against 2.4 s for a minute of 1440x1080.
--   + Fixed: on Windows the bottom rows of every frame were fingerprinted from the
--     padding a codec adds to reach whole macroblocks - a 1440x1080 file decodes as
--     1440x1088 - because the crop the format declares was ignored.
--   + Fixed: the ffmpeg fallback asked for "gray", which puts a scaler in the way that
--     expands video range to full range and clips both ends, moving hash bits. It now
--     takes the luma plane as the decoder produced it.
--   + Video fingerprint stores from before this change are read as stale and rebuilt,
--     since their numbers cannot be compared with new ones. Audio stores are unaffected.
--   + The diagnostics report no longer claims Windows decodes through ffmpeg.
--   + Fixed: cut detection on Windows found fewer cuts than macOS and put most of them
--     in the wrong place. Every Windows store was labelled 25 fps whatever the file
--     was, because the frame rate was read back off the output format rather than the
--     file's, and cut positions were worked out by dividing a frame number by it. So on
--     anything that was not 25 fps every cut moved, further the later it sat, and the
--     ones pushed past the end of the analysed stretch were dropped.
--   + Cut positions now come from the timestamp each frame carries instead of being
--     divided out of a frame rate, which is also correct for variable frame rate files
--     on either platform. The store's frame rate is measured from those timestamps
--     rather than taken on trust from the decoder.
--   + Fixed: a padded or bottom-up frame buffer on Windows was read as though its rows
--     were packed top-down, which fingerprinted rows of the wrong pixels and, for a
--     bottom-up buffer, read past the end of the buffer.
--   + VP_StoreInfo reports a store's frame count, declared and measured frame rate,
--     timestamp spacing and first hashes, for comparing one machine against another.
